rname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536110 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cb8adf3-5059-5402-b886-e9c6e0d73f1e" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536110 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{2t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}+\\frac{16{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2t}(2\\sin^{2}⁡4t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536116 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536153 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536116 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536116 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{3t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536116 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e7834dc-d7fc-52ab-b7ab-d76e42910958" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536116 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{3t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2({e}^{3t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j)\\cdot (3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3t}\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}(3\\sin^{2}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536131 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536108 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536131 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536131 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{4t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536131 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a59dfd2-3508-59f4-99f3-603cc9ac3e42" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536131 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{4t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{4t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)}{1+4({e}^{4t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{4t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\,j \\\\  & =4{e}^{4t}\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}\\,j)\\cdot (4{e}^{4t}\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{4t}\\sin^{2}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}+\\frac{12{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{4t}(4\\sin^{2}⁡3t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)}{1+4{e}^{8t}\\sin^{4}⁡3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186536133 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536134 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=x^{2} + x y^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,i+3 e^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "699d81bf-486b-5fcd-891b-b8329a36d705" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186536134 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + x y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x + y^{2})\\,i+2 x y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=3 e^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})+(3 e^{t})^{2})\\,i+2(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})(3 e^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(9 e^{2 t} + 2 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+6 e^{t} \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\,